      "rev_n_1_182221_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_182221_1",
        "node_id": "n_1_182221",
        "thought_text": "I need to refine the reasoning step to avoid the error in the code. The current approach of checking distinct characters and sorting by first occurrence is correct, but the implementation of creating the beautiful sequence needs to be adjusted. Instead of appending the entire remaining string q, we should append substrings from the start index to the end of the current index. This will ensure we capture all characters in the correct order. Additionally, we need to handle the case where k is greater than the number of distinct characters in q by printing 'NO' in such scenarios.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_1_182221_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_03e9677b53",
        "created_at": 1794529670.8635166
      },
      "rev_n_2_5156d8_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_5156d8_1",
        "node_id": "n_2_5156d8",
        "thought_text": "I apologize for the oversight. The error in the previous implementation was that the function didn't return any value, which caused the output mismatch. Let's refine the reasoning step to address this issue and improve the overall solution.\n\nInstead of trying to divide q into k substrings, we should focus on creating k distinct substrings that, when concatenated, form q. This approach ensures that we can handle all cases correctly, including when k is greater than the number of distinct characters in q.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_2_5156d8_0",
        "feedback_id": "fb_c16762c141",
        "created_at": 1794529702.3911426
      },
      "rev_n_3_eeaf44_1": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_eeaf44_1",
        "node_id": "n_3_eeaf44",
        "thought_text": "The current approach is close but has a flaw in handling cases where k is greater than the number of distinct starting characters in q. Instead of checking for equality, we should check if we have at least k strings. This allows us to create a beautiful sequence even when k is larger than the number of distinct starting characters, as long as we can form k non-overlapping substrings.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "5740354900026072187",
        "replaces_revision_id": "rev_n_3_eeaf44_0",
